  'ks.power': `{prov} today: {pop}, #{rank} of 68`, 'ks.voice': `Ask the guide to greet me`, 'ks.art': `Hiroshige painted your province: {title}`,
  'ks.note': `The seals show precision: "Town" is worked out for the municipality, "Dist." by Meiji-era district, "Pref." for the whole prefecture (dominant power), "Prov." by ancient province, "City" inferred from the districts of the whole city (all led by the same lord). Late-Edo lords come from the early-Meiji survey of villages and lords (via Japanese Wikipedia's district articles); where the seal says "Town", lords and their rice yields are summed village by village from the National Museum of Japanese History's database of that survey. Meiji villages are those the district articles say now lie here, filled in from the 1920 municipal boundaries of CODH's Historical Administrative Area Data Set where the articles are silent (villages merged away before 1920 are missing), and may be incomplete (names in Japanese). Ancient townships are from the 10th-century Wamyō Ruijushō (via the Nara National Research Institute for Cultural Properties' ancient place-name search), matched to the ancient district through the Meiji district only, not located individually. "Before mergers" overlays CODH's 1995 municipal boundaries and each municipality's dates of existence on today's municipalities by area ("part" where one straddles the border). Distances are straight-line.`,
